EXCLUSIVE: Amazon MGM Studios has inked an overall deal with Ullisses Campbell, the award-winning Brazilian journalist and crime writer, as it digs deeper into the true crime genre in Brazil.

Campbell is well-known in Brazil, and is one of the writers on Amazon MGM Studios&#x2019; prison drama Trememb&#xE9; , which has been a hit for Prime Video in Brazil. Now the studio has locked Campbell into a deal that will see him work across its whole true crime slate as well as on new projects.

&#x201C;Our country is home to extraordinary stories, many of which are stranger than fiction,&#x201D; said Campbell. &#x201C;Brazil's history is also told through its crimes. Many of these cases reflect the customs, prejudices, inequalities and social shifts of their time. When screen adaptations revisit these events, they also create a historical record and help preserve our collective memory. That is why I see true crime as a lens to understand who we are and how we got here.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Everything We Know About Amazon MGM & Sony Pictures TV's 'God Of War' Adaptation So Far Casting 'Upgraded 2' Film Casts Tanner Zagarino, Christopher McDonald, Andrea Arcangeli & Alessandro Preziosi

Campbell wrote a series of books about Trememb&#xE9; , a real-life prison that has housed some of Brazil&#x2019;s most notorious criminals. Inmates have included Suzane von Richthofen, Elize Matsunaga and the Cravinhos brothers. The drama series Trememb&#xE9; , which Campbell co-wrote with Vera Egito, Juliana Rosenthal, Thays Berbe and Maria Isabel Iorio, is heading into Season 2. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

&#x201C;It is a franchise, definitely,&#x201D; Julia Priolli, Head of Creative for Brazilian Originals at Amazon MGM Studios, told Deadline about expanding the Trememb&#xE9; IP. She added there will be some infamous new inmates in the upcoming run of the show. &#x201C;For Season 2, we're bringing five new characters, including Robinho, the football player [imprisoned after being convicted of rape].&#x201D;

Campbell will exec produce Season 2 of Trememb&#xE9; , which launches in 2027. Speaking more broadly about the Amazon MGM deal with the journalist, writer and producer, Priolli said he will have input into the true crime docs, series and films the studio is working on. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;It could be as an exec producer, or sometimes it will be doing research, because he's very strong when you need to go deep into aspects of a story and he really has great access,&#x201D; she said. &#x201C;It doesn't matter if we're talking about a documentary, or if it's a movie or a scripted series, we want his input across everything. We're talking to him about some new shows that we haven't announced yet, but basically, we want him across the whole true crime slate.&#x201D;

Priolli explained that Amazon has linked up its production, streaming and publishing businesses in Brazil as it pushes deeper into ripped from the headlines crime content.

&#x201C;We tried to identify, here in Brazil, what would be the best true crime stories for us and we wanted to use the whole flywheel that we have at Amazon for telling these stories. We not only have the studios where we could do a movie or a documentary or a scripted series, but we also have Audible, where we can do those as audio, we have Kindle, we have Amazon Books.&#x201D;

Long before making &#x201C;52 Blue,&#x201D; a film about a young man from India who travels to Qatar hoping to meet Lionel Messi during the World Cup, Egyptian filmmaker Ali El Arabi had a similarly ambitious dream of becoming a world champion boxer and meeting Muhammad Ali.

&#x201C;My dad had a lot of worries and fear from the world. He wanted me to stay in the village,&#x201D; El Arabi tells Variety at the Doha Film Festival , where the film is having a homecoming of sorts. &#x201C;I wanted to run away to meet Muhammad Ali.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Amazon Debuts $30 Streaming Bundle That Includes Starz, BritBox, AMC+, MGM+ and PBS Masterpiece In One Place Celebrity Streaming and Commerce Platform TalkShopLive Expands to TikTok Ahead of Black Friday

With his mother&#x2019;s help, El Arabi left his village and joined the national kickboxing team, winning his father&#x2019;s approval only after appearing on television. His experience echoes through &#x201C;52 Blue,&#x201D; a story about family expectations, loneliness and the freedom to forge one&#x2019;s own path.

The idea emerged while El Arabi was working on a music video for the 2022 FIFA World Cup in Qatar. There, he met Ashish, a worker from Kerala, India, who mistook him for a footballer and asked for a photograph with him to send to his mother. Their encounter inspired the film&#x2019;s protagonist and drew El Arabi&#x2019;s attention to the untold stories of workers preparing for the tournament.

The film takes its title from 52 Blue, an individual whale whose unusual calls earned it the nickname of the world&#x2019;s loneliest whale, though it has never been sighted. For El Arabi, its isolation reflects both his childhood and his protagonist&#x2019;s experiences.

&#x201C;This whale is me, and it&#x2019;s Ashish too,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;Everyone wants to be part of the world through their own experience. Each of us deserves to have our own experience, on our own terms.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

El Arabi, whose documentary &#x201C; Captains of Zaatari &#x201D; followed two Syrian refugees pursuing their football dreams, drew on that background to blur the boundaries between fiction and documentary. He shot during the actual World Cup, immersing lead actor Yadav Shashidhar among real stadium workers who believed they were participating in a documentary.

&#x201C;I didn&#x2019;t tell the workers that we were making a fiction film, give them directions, or give them or Yadav a script [for those scenes],&#x201D; he explains. &#x201C;I&#x2019;m just filming how he&#x2019;s blending in and melting in with them. I took all of this material to create my scenes.&#x201D;

El Arabi filmed one three-hour conversation among workers with four cameras, later shaping their exchanges into one of the film&#x2019;s most powerful scenes. &#x201C;Who am I to change the reality of these people?&#x201D; he ponders. &#x201C;I wanted to capture that reality and bring it into the film.&#x201D;

Filming during the tournament required considerable improvisation as the crew faced limited stadium access and little opportunity for retakes. &#x201C;We didn&#x2019;t have a shooting list. We didn&#x2019;t have much time. We had a maximum of 100 minutes during each match,&#x201D; he recalls. &#x201C;We didn&#x2019;t have the permissions necessary to really be everywhere since FIFA has a lot of rules.&#x201D;

For Shashidhar, whose previous credits included smaller roles in Indian films, Ashish&#x2019;s obsession with Messi was rooted in loneliness. Having lost his brother, his character struggles to connect with his father.

&#x201C;He was not able to express himself to his father. He wanted him to understand him, but that was not happening,&#x201D; Shashidhar says. &#x201C;He was always searching for companionship.&#x201D;

Although he was never an avid football fan himself, Shashidhar drew inspiration from the passionate Messi and Ronaldo supporters he knew growing up in Kerala, where enormous cutouts of football stars appear during tournaments. He also turned to an unusual source to understand Ashish&#x2019;s emotional journey: the animated film &#x201C;Finding Nemo.&#x201D;

&#x201C;People will die for these players, even if they are not from the same nation,&#x201D; he says of football&#x2019;s global fandom. &#x201C;That&#x2019;s one thing which unites them, no matter what community they come from.&#x201D;

For El Arabi, Ashish&#x2019;s quest is about something more fundamental than meeting his idol. &#x201C;He ran away from his family, from his life to meet Messi,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;But actually he ran away to meet himself and to meet the world.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The filmmaker sees Ashish&#x2019;s struggle for independence as one he has faced both in sports and in cinema. &#x201C;It&#x2019;s not just about families. It&#x2019;s about anyone who can control us,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;Anyone who feels they can judge us, tell us what we can&#x2019;t achieve or impose limits on our lives.&#x201D;

&#x201C;My dream is for filmmakers and the younger generation to have a voice without anyone telling them what to say,&#x201D; he adds.

El Arabi is now completing his next feature, &#x201C;2-1,&#x201D; a music-driven drama that marks his first Egyptian film. He revealed the new title exclusively to Variety . The project was previously known as &#x201C;Watar Wahed,&#x201D; meaning &#x201C;One String&#x201D; in Arabic.

The film stars Egyptian rapper Wegz, making his feature acting debut, alongside Asser Yassin , Tara Emad and Mayan El Sayed. It follows twin brothers, one deaf and the other a successful rapper. When the musician enters rehab, his brother assumes his identity onstage. Yassin plays the rapper&#x2019;s manager.

Currently in post-production, the film, produced by Ambient Light and Film Square, is tentatively slated for an Egyptian theatrical release in early 2027.

&#x201C;It&#x2019;s about music. It&#x2019;s about history, too. It&#x2019;s about love. It&#x2019;s about a success story,&#x201D; El Arabi beams. &#x201C;This is the first Egyptian movie for me. And this is the biggest one, I believe.&#x201D;

In the most transformative cases, hallucinogenic drugs can come with a troubling personal revelation and debilitating comedown. For iLoveMakonnen, they came with a relocation : During a &#x201C;psychedelic journey&#x201D; about 10 years ago, while considering a move from New York City, he noticed his pet dogs chomping on a chew toy bearing an Oregon symbol.

&#x201C;I&#x2019;m like, &#x2018;This must be a sign,&#x2019;&#x201D; he tells Variety over Zoom. Luckily, the cosmic signal aligned with the practical one: &#x201C;I looked it up. &#x2018;Oh shit, they got some shit I could afford there. I'm out.&#x2019;&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Anthony Boyle on Becoming Sam Bankman-Fried for ‘The Altruists’ and Finding the Grey in History’s Villains: ‘I’ve Played More Real People Than Not’ Netflix Orders Harlan Coben Limited Series 'The Innocent' From 'I Will Find You' Team

Following his relocation to his current home of Portland, one could say he never fully returned. After breaking out with his drowsily melodic single &#x201C;Tuesday&#x201D; and signing with Drake&#x2019;s OVO Records in 2014, Makonnen became both an emblem of late blog-era spontaneity and a cautionary tale of maze-like industry politics. His sound &#x2014; a narcotized flurry of dazed melody and weirdo drug raps &#x2014; made him a forebear for a generation of eclectic SoundCloud rappers like Lil Yachty and others. He might be the only rapper who&#x2019;s ever collaborated with YoungBoy Never Broke Again, Drake and the late Lil Peep, who called Makonnen an influence. He was also one of the first mainstream rappers to come out as a gay man.

His voice and aesthetic brought him into the spotlight, but after a series of half-hearted retirements and at least one cross-country move, he seems to be mostly cool being out of it. In Portland, Makonnen, who says he&#x2019;s looking to move to Atlanta in the near future, has gotten used to life away from the chaos of the music industry scene in the Big Apple. Things look very different &#x2014; they&#x2019;re peaceful. Quieter. A little more lonely. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;I'm not here for socializing with people,&#x201D; he says. On the days he does interact with Portlanders, he finds them to be quirky and inclined to keep to themselves. Free of obvious celebrity, the Makonnen of 2026 enjoys walks through Laurelhurst Park or Unthank Park. If he&#x2019;s not working or taking a walk, he&#x2019;s chatting it up with an AI bot, the ultimate judgment-free foil. &#x201C;[It&#x2019;s] just [me] coming up with crazy concepts in my head that other humans would usually shoot down and tell me I'm crazy,&#x201D; he says. When he&#x2019;s not doing that, he&#x2019;s stocking shelves at a local supermarket. And he&#x2019;s enjoying it. After all, what&#x2019;s not to like about giving people the supplies they need?

When I ask Makonnen how his shelf-stocking skills compare to any coworkers, he brushes off the notion of making any comparisons. But he takes pride in the work. &#x201C;I just want to be more helpful to my community,&#x201D; he says. Makonnen might not be hunting for national attention, but when he posts social media videos of himself on the job, he gets it.

In August 2025, he appeared in TikTok videos of himself flipping burgers at Little Griddle during a fundraiser for fire-damaged Burn Money Studios. The viral clout &#x2014; one work video earned over 1.3 million TikTok views &#x2014; boosted its patronage, although Makonnen says his popularity eventually interfered with its regular operations. But for Makonnen, the work is more than just Internet novelty or pocket change he can spend on occasional gambling trips to Las Vegas.

&#x201C;I like to take on the other role, the other side. If everybody looks at me as if I'm some rich man, I want to see what it looks like [if I&#x2019;m seen as] a peasant,&#x201D; he says. Outside that layer of perceptual experimentation, there&#x2019;s something more existential at play. &#x201C;I'm feeling useful as a human again,&#x201D; he adds. &#x201C;I'm not useful in music anymore.&#x201D;

If Makonnen feels like he no longer fits into the hip-hop landscape, that clearly hasn&#x2019;t stopped him from making a lot of music. Dating back to 2021, he&#x2019;s dropped intermittent spurts of inspired projects laced with glitchy electronic synths and conflicting feelings. His expanded catalog has never reached the commercial heights of his earlier work, but it&#x2019;s distinct enough that it's a shock to hear he isn't receiving offers for label deals or, really, much of anything. Even his slot as a co-headliner at "Pride in Demand: Portland Queer Takeover" felt like a grim reminder of a clout deficiency. &#x201C;It was one of my least-attended shows ever,&#x201D; he says. It&#x2019;s just one example of what he says is a problem that shouldn&#x2019;t be one: &#x201C;I'm gay, I'm colored. I'm from America, [I&#x2019;ve] got a Grammy-nominated song. It's like, [I] fit in so many markets where a lot of people can't go. And it's like, &#x2018;We still can't book you.&#x2019;&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

It&#x2019;s unclear how much Makonnen still wants stardom. But tracks like his newly released single &#x201C;Lie 2 Me&#x201D; suggest an enduring talent. The track, which features longtime collaborator and rapper-producer Key! (21 Savage, Playboi Carti), is framed in new-wave synths and emotions that flutter as violently as his supple tenor. For the track, Makonnen sings his way through romantic turmoil: &#x201C;Kiss me like you mean it tomorrow / Not just when we&#x2019;re fucked up and the lights low.&#x201D; In his own words, it&#x2019;s situationship music. For a man who fell out of love with the rigors of the music industry, it&#x2019;s also a course correction. &#x201C;I enjoy making music with Key!&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;It feels like therapy. It doesn't feel like a job.&#x201D;

Makonnen&#x2019;s new Key! collaboration, which belongs to their forthcoming joint album, &#x201C;Over It,&#x201D; feels like a return to more innocent times, before rap became a career and the notion of him working a day job was a worst-case scenario. But the truth is, he&#x2019;s already lived through a haze of bad outcomes: the death of his friend and collaborator Lil Peep; conversations with estranged, seriously ill relatives who finally managed to tell him they were proud. &#x201C;I'm hearing a lot of apologies, maybe 15 years too late,&#x201D; he says.

He&#x2019;s more isolated in Portland, but there&#x2019;s a clarity that comes with it: &#x201C;I feel like I'm alone. I'm on Earth. This is my thing. If I'm not throwing the party at night, inviting everybody, nobody's showing the fuck up.&#x201D; That feeling extends to his place in hip-hop, too, where he believes coming out as gay led to his ostracization. As far as we&#x2019;ve come, the rap world still doesn&#x2019;t welcome the LGBTQ+ community as readily as it should. And so his own decision to come out wasn&#x2019;t just about him. &#x201C;Look at how many gay artists there are now, and look at how much they&#x2019;re fucking with all this,&#x201D; he says. &#x201C;I'm doing this for the kids after me. I'm not doing this for me.&#x201D;

The thing is, there&#x2019;s a downside to that honesty: &#x201C;The only regret is being real, because the world ain't real.&#x201D; And even if it was, Makonnen says he knows it wouldn&#x2019;t necessarily get him a trophy. &#x201C;Being real is not awarded&#x2026; I'm not gonna be able to be a star&#x2026; so I'm gonna go do my real life.&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

As an early architect of a sound he never got to fully cash in on and an openly queer artist who emerged in an era that wasn't ready to fully accept him, Makonnen is used to building things he might never get to &#x2014; or never choose to &#x2014; enjoy.

&#x201C;Before I was a celebrity, I've always experienced being in the service industry,&#x201D; he tells me. &#x201C;So I know about servicing more than being serviced.&#x201D;

EXCLUSIVE: Sabrina S. Gordon and Oscar-nominated actress Danielle Brooks have joined the award-winning short documentary Free Joan Little as executive producers.

The Oscar-contending film, directed by Yoruba Richen , &#x201C;recounts the groundbreaking 1975 case of a young Black woman in eastern North Carolina who killed a white jailer after he attempted to rape her,&#x201D; according to a synopsis. &#x201C;Little&#x2019;s flight from custody and the ensuing media frenzy galvanized a nationwide movement for her cause, with support from civil rights icons Angela Davis and Rosa Parks.&#x201D;

